{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"ETF arbitrage keeps its market price near its net asset value (NAV). If an ETF trades above the value of its holdings, authorized participants can create new shares—typically by delivering the underlying basket—and sell them at the higher market price. That added supply tends to push the ETF price down. If it trades below NAV, they can buy ETF shares, redeem them for the underlying basket, and profit from the difference; buying pressure tends to lift the price.\n\nThis mechanism is strongest when the ETF and its holdings are liquid and markets are open. Prices can diverge more when underlying assets are hard to trade or markets are closed, during volatility, or when creation/redemption is constrained."}}
